The license in its crux is a simple, permissive blend of the BSD-0 and BSD-2
licenses, where the copyright is both implicit and optional, until (in cases
where the BSD-2 functionality is desired) directly supplied and thus clarified
in the license file itself.

Here is a detailed breakdown of the peculiarities and any differences:

(1) The license name hints at a CLA (Contributor License Agreement) document,
    an ancillary part of licensing. KEYCLA specifically, due to its licensing
    conditions, can accommodate two very simple forms of CLA. And in fact, for
    convenience, they have already been crafted and included as ready-to-use
    templates, in the license source directory.

(2) The license disclaimer is noticeably short compared to either BSD-0/BSD-2,
    mostly blending seamlessly with any general license disclaimer text, while
    remaining completely identical in function and intent. In practice,
    concerning the binary clause, shipping any general disclaimer text
    will satisfy the requirement of the license to redistribute the disclaimer.

(3) The license omits 'this list of conditions' as a sub requirement in
    the binary clause. In comparison, the BSD-2 does state that; in practice,
    the requirement is satisfied and respected by shipping the license text.
    The omission in KEYCLA therefore serves to remove any such requirement
    whatsoever.
